<suitelet scriptid="customscript_mcgi_sl_arch_order_create">
  <description>CWP ARCH sales-order creation write path. Creates the Sales Order from the trader screen's wizard, with each line carrying its lot in inventory detail so the bundle leaves availability on save. Runs as customrole2184 because the executing role is what decides which SO form is used, and only a form with Inventory Detail enabled can carry a lot; see the runasrole comment on the deployment. Authorisation is enforced in script against custscript_arch_order_roles, which fails closed to Administrator only when unset.</description>
  <isinactive>F</isinactive>
  <name>MCGI SL ARCH Order Create</name>
  <!-- admins=F matches every other script in this account. This endpoint is hit
       by traders, and it already handles expected refusals itself, returning
       JSON and logging them at AUDIT. Emailing all administrators on top of that
       is noise, and noise is how real failures get ignored. -->
  <notifyadmins>F</notifyadmins>
  <notifyemails>aserban@houseblend.io</notifyemails>
  <!-- Notifications go to Andrei ONLY while ARCH is being built. notifyowner=T
       sends them to the script owner, "House Blend 2"
       (credentials.ns_mcg2@houseblend.io) — a shared HouseBlend credentials
       mailbox, so the person actually building this never sees them.
       notifyadmins stays F: MGSL admins must not be paged by our in-progress
       work. -->
  <notifyowner>F</notifyowner>
  <notifyuser>F</notifyuser>
  <scriptfile>[/SuiteScripts/mcgi_services/trader_screen/entry_points/sl/mcgi_sl_arch_order_create.js]</scriptfile>
  <scriptcustomfields>
    <scriptcustomfield scriptid="custscript_arch_order_roles">
      <accesslevel>2</accesslevel>
      <applyformatting>F</applyformatting>
      <checkspelling>F</checkspelling>
      <defaultchecked>F</defaultchecked>
      <defaultselection></defaultselection>
      <defaultvalue></defaultvalue>
      <description>Comma separated role internal IDs permitted to create an ARCH sales order from the trader screen. Empty means Administrator only, which is the safe default for a deployment that writes with elevated rights. Administrator is always retained even if omitted from the list, so a bad entry cannot lock out the person who has to fix it. This is DELIBERATELY a separate parameter from custscript_arch_split_roles: creating an order is a lower-privilege act than posting an inventory adjustment, so this list will end up wider, and sharing one list would force whoever widens this to also widen adjustment-posting rights.</description>
      <displayheight></displayheight>
      <displaytype>NORMAL</displaytype>
      <displaywidth></displaywidth>
      <dynamicdefault></dynamicdefault>
      <fieldtype>TEXT</fieldtype>
      <help>Example: 1057,1063. Empty is safe and means Administrator only.</help>
      <isformula>F</isformula>
      <ismandatory>F</ismandatory>
      <label>Permitted Order Roles</label>
      <linktext></linktext>
      <maxlength></maxlength>
      <maxvalue></maxvalue>
      <minvalue></minvalue>
      <onparentdelete></onparentdelete>
      <searchlevel>2</searchlevel>
      <selectrecordtype></selectrecordtype>
      <setting></setting>
      <storevalue>T</storevalue>
    </scriptcustomfield>
    <scriptcustomfield scriptid="custscript_arch_insurance_rate">
      <accesslevel>2</accesslevel>
      <applyformatting>F</applyformatting>
      <checkspelling>F</checkspelling>
      <defaultchecked>F</defaultchecked>
      <defaultselection></defaultselection>
      <defaultvalue>0.003</defaultvalue>
      <description>Operations and insurance rate stamped onto ARCH sales orders as a fraction of material cost. Measured across all 4,216 sales orders in this account on 2026-08-20: 3,979 (94.4%) carry 0.003, the range is 0.0015 to 0.015 and 48 are null. Configured here rather than accepted from the browser, for the same reason the split endpoint stopped accepting a GL account: a screen must not choose financial context. Leave at 0.003 unless the client states otherwise. Values outside 0 to 1 are ignored in favour of the 0.003 default.</description>
      <displayheight></displayheight>
      <displaytype>NORMAL</displaytype>
      <displaywidth></displaywidth>
      <dynamicdefault></dynamicdefault>
      <fieldtype>FLOAT</fieldtype>
      <help>Fraction, not a percentage. 0.003 means 0.3%.</help>
      <isformula>F</isformula>
      <ismandatory>F</ismandatory>
      <label>Ops and Insurance Rate</label>
      <linktext></linktext>
      <maxlength></maxlength>
      <maxvalue></maxvalue>
      <minvalue></minvalue>
      <onparentdelete></onparentdelete>
      <searchlevel>2</searchlevel>
      <selectrecordtype></selectrecordtype>
      <setting></setting>
      <storevalue>T</storevalue>
    </scriptcustomfield>
  </scriptcustomfields>
  <scriptdeployments>
    <scriptdeployment scriptid="customdeploy_mcgi_sl_arch_order_create">
      <custscript_arch_insurance_rate>0.003</custscript_arch_insurance_rate>
      <allemployees>F</allemployees>
      <allpartners>F</allpartners>
      <allroles>F</allroles>
      <!-- Administrator only, until the trader role is settled with the client.
           allemployees=F is not by itself enough: an admin does NOT bypass a
           Suitelet audience, so with no role listed the endpoint refuses
           everyone including the person who has to test it. Add the trader role
           HERE as well as to custscript_arch_order_roles when it is known; the
           audience decides who can reach the URL, the parameter decides who the
           script will act for. -->
      <audslctrole>ADMINISTRATOR</audslctrole>
      <eventtype></eventtype>
      <isdeployed>T</isdeployed>
      <isonline>F</isonline>
      <loglevel>DEBUG</loglevel>
      <!-- 🔴 runasrole IS LOAD-BEARING. It is not a security setting here, it is
           what decides whether an order can carry its lot at all.

           `customform` cannot be set from a server script in this account
           (MODULE_DOES_NOT_EXIST on setValue/save, for every form), so the form
           comes from the EXECUTING ROLE's preference. Inventory Detail is a
           per-form column: the globally preferred Industriel form has it OFF, so
           an Administrator-as-role deployment produces orders that silently carry
           no lots.

           customrole2184 "MGSL - CWP MTL - Hardwood AP/AR Analyst" is used because
           it is the only kind of role that satisfies BOTH halves:
             - the ARCH form (386) is preferred for it, so Inventory Detail exists
             - subsidiaryoption=SELECTED covers subsidiary 5, where hardwood lives
             - Sales Order = EDIT, so it can create

           ⚠️ Why NOT customrole2181, "CWP ARC - Trader", which is the role the
           ARCH form was built for. It is subsidiaryoption=OWN, meaning it sees the
           EXECUTING USER's subsidiary rather than a fixed list. That makes it
           caller-dependent: it works for a trader in subsidiary 5, where hardwood
           lives, and silently sees nothing for a caller anywhere else. Our own
           integration user is in subsidiary 1, which is exactly how this was
           found — reads returned ZERO ROWS instead of erroring, and every order
           was refused with a misleading "lot has no stock at that location any
           more".

           ⚠️ CORRECTION worth keeping, because the first read of this was wrong:
           2181 is not broken and an ARCH trader is not blind. Employee 3293
           "Trader Hardwood", the one account holding that role, IS in subsidiary
           5 and would see the wood. The problem is only that OWN makes visibility
           depend on who calls.

           2184 is chosen because subsidiaryoption=SELECTED is a FIXED list, so
           this endpoint behaves identically no matter which user reaches it. For
           a server endpoint that is the property that matters. Verified
           2026-08-20 across all ten roles the ARCH form is preferred for.

           NOT Administrator, despite the split endpoint using it: that one posts
           Inventory Adjustments, which a trader cannot. Creating a sales order
           needs only Sales Order permission. The role allowlist in the script is
           still what governs WHO may call this. -->
      <runasrole>[scriptid=customrole2184]</runasrole>
      <status>RELEASED</status>
      <title>MCGI SL ARCH Order Create</title>
    </scriptdeployment>
  </scriptdeployments>
</suitelet>
